UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 35, Lesson 1: Project planning and timeline', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Year 7 Technology Mandatory develops design and practical judgement for project planning and timeline. Students identify needs, generate ideas against criteria, work safely, test, and evaluate improvements. Catalogue alignment uses TM4-1. Follow classroom safety rules for tools, food and digital work.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nFor project planning and timeline, complete: need → criteria → idea/sketch or plan → safety check → one evaluation note.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: criteria clear; idea matches need; safety/sustainability considered; evaluation honest.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSkipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently complete a substantial task on project planning and timeline: document the design/practical process with criteria, evidence of making or planning, a test/result, and a five-sentence evaluation with one improvement.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Technology","learning_intention":"Year 7 Technology Mandatory develops design and practical judgement for project planning and timeline. Students identify needs, generate ideas against criteria, work safely, test, and evaluate improvements. Catalogue alignment uses TM4-1. Follow classroom safety rules for tools, food and digital work.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["TM4-1"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently complete a substantial task on project planning and timeline: document the design/practical process with criteria, evidence of making or planning, a test/result, and a five-sentence evaluation with one impr Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using project on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing.. Correction: Strong answer: criteria clear;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating project on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with project, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about project with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for project. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for project. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing.","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","criteria clear; idea matches need; safety/sustainability considered; evaluation honest."],"question":"For project planning and timeline, complete: need → criteria → idea/sketch or plan → safety check → one evaluation note. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for project; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["criteria clear; idea matches need; safety/sustainability considered; evaluation honest.","Careful use of planning with clear evidence.","Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing.","Year 7 Technology Mandatory develops design and practical judgement for project planning and timeline."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with project?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named planning, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name planning only, without linking it to project.","Apply project to new material and name the planning used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing."],"question":"Which response best applies project with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on project. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing.","Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing.","criteria clear; idea matches need; safety/sustainability considered; evaluation honest.","Start making with no criteria or safety check."],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing project?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing.","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves project.","Start making with no criteria or safety check.","Call the first attempt finished without testing."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about project?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Start making with no criteria or safety check.","Call the first attempt finished without testing.","Example for Project planning and timeline: state the need/problem, list success criteria, propose one idea, and note","Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Year 7 Technology Mandatory develops design and practical judgement for project planning and — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue."],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for project?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Independently complete a substantial task on project planning and timeline: document the design/practical process with","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing.","Name planning only, with no linked example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies project to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about project with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating project on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using project on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for project. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with project, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing.. Correction: Strong answer: criteria clear;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-tech-y7-w35-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 NESA Technology Mandatory alignment using catalogue outcome codes (TM4-*); content-point mapping remains pending. Practical work requires teacher safety supervision.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise project.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Design","success_criteria":["I can use project with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify planning and explain why it matters.","I can practise timeline independently on a new prompt.","I can notice evaluate needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Year 7 Technology Mandatory develops design and practical judgement for project planning and timeline.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Year 7 Technology Mandatory develops design and practical judgement for project planning and timeline.","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about project?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample for Project planning and timeline: state the need/problem, list success criteria, propose one idea, and note one safety or sustainability check. Weak: jumping to make without criteria.\n\nTRY FIRST\nFor project planning and timeline, complete: need → criteria → idea/sketch or plan → safety check → one evaluation note.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: criteria clear; idea matches need; safety/sustainability considered; evaluation honest.","explain":"Year 7 Technology Mandatory develops design and practical judgement for project planning and timeline. Students identify needs, generate ideas against criteria, work safely, test, and evaluate improvements. Catalogue alignment uses TM4-1. Follow classroom safety rules for tools, food and digital work.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Skipping criteria and safety for project planning and timeline, or calling a first attempt finished without testing. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Project planning and timeline.","caption":"Plan before make.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Need</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Project planning and timeline</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Criteria</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">success checks</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the i